{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Channel strip: HPF/LPF, gate, analog EQ, compressor, saturation, limiter, true peak metering. DSP ports of the original plugins (not of the standalone versions): HPF/LPF: Ported from x42/fil4.lv2 (Robin Gareus). GPLv2+. https://github.com/x42/fil4.lv2 Gate: Ported from ZamGateX2 (zam-plugins). GPLv2+. https://github.com/zamaudio/zam-plugins Analog EQ: Ported from Luftikus (lkjb). MIT. https://github.com/lkjbdsp/lkjb-plugins/tree/master/Luftikus Compressor: Ported from x42/darc.lv2 (Robin Gareus). GPLv2+. https://github.com/x42/darc.lv2 Saturation: DSP from Airwindows ChannelX by Chris Johnson (MIT License). https://github.com/airwindows/airwindows/tree/master/plugins/LinuxVST/src/ChannelX Limiter & True Peak metering: Ported from x42/dpl.lv2 (Robin Gareus; DSP by Fons Adriaensen). GPLv3+. https://github.com/x42/dpl.lv2 JSFX port by chmaha. GPLv3. Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 General MIDI synthesizer with 16 multitimbral channels, 128 GM instruments and a drum kit, up to 256 voices (default 64), the original reverb and chorus (per-channel CC91/CC93 sends or the whole mix), GM/master-volume SysEx, A4 tuning and 80 historical temperaments (as in Sonata Chamber Organ) on the melodic channels. Ported from the Sonivox EAS wavetable synthesizer (the Android MIDI synth, Sonic Network Inc. and The Android Open Source Project), including its built-in GM sound bank https://github.com/pedrolcl/sonivox (Apache License 2.0) The engine runs at 44.1 kHz with the original integer arithmetic and matches it sample for sample; other host rates are resampled. Original DSP Copyright (c) 2004-2006 Sonic Network Inc. Licensed under the Apache License v2: http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0 JSFX code released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Oscilloscope with XY mode, time and scale controls, hold, sync, grid and colour settings. Click the scope to open or close the settings panel. Ported from unplugred's Scope (https://github.com/unplugred/vsts), AGPLv3+. Image assets by unplugred, CC BY-NC (see Dependencies/Scope/Scope-LICENSE.txt). Claude Sonnet 5.x was involved in the porting of the DSP and GUI JSFX code released under AGPLv3+ license\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Sample quality reduction: lower the bit depth and sample rate, add harmonic "thickening", clip the peaks and muffle the result with a steep low-pass filter. Starts clean (24 bits, full rate, no thickening, filter off) so you can dial the damage in. Turn Rate left of centre to hold single samples (gritty, aliased) or right to hold averaged samples (smoother); turn Non-Lin left for even harmonics or right for odd harmonics. Mono sums the input like the original plugin; Stereo degrades each channel on its own. Ported from mda-lv2 by Paul Kellett (Maxim Digital Audio) and David Robillard https://gitlab.com/drobilla/mda-lv2 (GPLv3+) JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A physically modelled reel-to-reel tape machine. The heart of it is a magnetic hysteresis model of the tape itself (Drive, Saturation and Bias), solved with your choice of Runge-Kutta or Newton-Raphson solvers, a small neural network (STN) or the original v1 sound, and oversampled up to 16x. Around it sit the rest of the machine: input filters, tone, a tape-style compressor, playback head loss (speed, spacing, thickness, gap and azimuth, with the head bump), wow and flutter, degradation noise and "chew" for damaged tape, plus mid/side and stereo balance. Comes with the 94 factory presets. Needs Dependencies/ReelTalk/stn_weights.txt for the STN tape mode. Ported from CHOW Tape Model by Jatin Chowdhury (Chowdhury DSP) https://github.com/jatinchowdhury18/AnalogTapeModel (GPLv3) JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 General MIDI and GS workstation playing S. Christian Collins' GeneralUser GS 2.0.3 through a port of FluidSynth: Ported from FluidSynth 2.6.1 (Peter Hanappe and others, https://www.fluidsynth.org, GNU LGPL 2.1): Sound set: GeneralUser GS 2.0.3 by S. Christian Collins (https://www.schristiancollins.com), GeneralUser GS License v2.0 (Dependencies/ScoringSynth/ScoringSynth-LICENSE.txt). JSFX code released under the GNU LGPL 2.1 or later. Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An audio-reactive visualizer with twenty-five scenes. Plasma: flowing colour fields that keep a steady pace. The louder and busier the music, the more vivid the colours and the finer the pattern breaks up. Stereo width adds a second swirl. Lava Lamp: eight glowing blobs, each one following its own part of the spectrum, from sub-bass to air. Nebula: a feedback tunnel with a spectrum ring and a waveform ring that zooms on kicks and turns with the mids. Kaleidoscope: six mirrored slices of a flowing pattern that zoom on bass and kicks and turn with the mids. Tunnel: a race down a glowing tunnel. Loudness sets the speed and shifts the colour, and the track bends left or right as the music gets brighter or darker. Starfield: a warp-speed flight. Bass and kicks push the throttle and each star glows with its own band. Ridgelines: a scrolling 3D landscape of the spectrum, newest at the front. Aurora: curtains of light over a dark horizon, taller where the spectrum is louder (bass in the middle, treble at the sides). Fireworks: every kick bursts a big shell, in the colour of the loudest band, right on the hit. Snares and hi-hats add small crackles. Without drums, note onsets burst shells sized by their strength. Silk: hundreds of threads drift through a flowing field, each one carried by its own band. Phosphor: a glowing stereo vectorscope (mid up, side across), mirrored three ways into a slowly turning mandala. Stained Glass: sixteen drifting panes of coloured glass, each one lit by its own band. Julia: a morphing Julia-set fractal. The mids steer its shape, bass and kicks zoom in. Cymatics: sand gathers on the nodal lines of a vibrating plate. The dominant pitch picks the mode, so each new note re-forms the pattern, and the louder the music the more the sand dances. Skyline: a city of spectrum bars with lit windows and slowly falling rooftop beacons, reflected in the water. Chroma Wheel: twelve petals, one per pitch class, round the circle of fifths. Chords light up their own shape. Live Score: a full 88-key piano. Every key that sounds releases coloured smoke, which rises, curls into eddies, mixes with its neighbours and thins out as it climbs. Choose the Smoke look (lit from the keys, turning to grey wisps) or the Paint look (glossy colour that never fades) in the settings. Constellations: each note onset lights a star at the height of its pitch, and phrases join up into constellations drifting across the sky. Bloom: a flower of sixteen petal rings, from bass at the centre to treble at the edge, each breathing with its band. Digital Rain: streams of glowing glyphs pour down, bass in the middle and treble at the sides. Louder bands rain faster, longer and brighter, and every hit or note onset sends down fresh streams. Slime: a living slime mould. Thousands of agents lay glowing trails and follow each other into branching veins. Loudness drives them, the mids change how they branch, and hits scatter them. Galaxy: a spiral galaxy seen by a slowly orbiting camera that drifts closer when the music is loud. Stars glow with their bands (bass at the core, treble at the rim) among gas clouds and dust lanes. Note onsets ignite new star-forming knots, kicks send a shockwave out along the arms, and the biggest hits set off a supernova. Synthwave: a striped sun that swells with the bass, spectrum mountains and a neon grid that races on loud music. Plasma Globe: lightning tendrils crackle from the core to the glass, more of them as the music builds. Harmonograph: pendulum line art. The ratio of the swings follows the interval being played, so a fifth draws a different figure from a third. Click the picture to open or close the settings. In Nebula and Starfield, hi-hats and snares throw sparks. Without drums (classical, ambient), note onsets take over the drum reactions, and loudness is judged against the recent level so quiet passages stay alive. Audio passes through untouched. Original design by chmaha JSFX code released under GPLv3 license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SoundFont (SF2) General MIDI synthesizer with 16 multitimbral channels playing HL4MGM, FluidSynth's reverb engines (FDN, Freeverb, Dattorro) and chorus, per-channel level, pan and Follows layering, A4 tuning and 80 historical temperaments on the melodic channels (as in Major General MIDI), interpolation from none to 25-point sinc, and GM, GM2, GS and XG reset SysEx. Ported from FluidSynth 2.6.1 (Peter Hanappe and others, https://www.fluidsynth.org, GNU LGPL 2.1): sample playback, envelopes, LFOs, resonant filter, SoundFont modulators, voice allocation, MIDI handling, the FDN and Freeverb reverbs and the chorus match FluidSynth sample for sample. Sound set: HL4MGM 0.9.0 RC1 by Stardream Studios (strixSF2, Yingchun Soul), a 4 MB bank for Sound Blaster and AWE cards based on Yingchun Soul's 2MGM (samples from VCSL, FreePats, Iowa MIS, OSDK), Public Domain. JSFX code released under the GNU LGPL 2.1 or later. Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Spaghetti Junction MIDI Filters: a modular rack for the 34 x42 MIDI filters. Insert up to eight filters as blocks and patch them with cables from MIDI IN to MIDI OUT. An output can feed several inputs (parallel branches) and an input merges every cable plugged into it, so serial chains, key splits and layers are all possible. Right-click the canvas (or use ADD FILTER) to insert a filter after the selected block, or at the end of the chain when nothing is selected (hold Shift to insert it unpatched). Drag between an output jack and an input jack, in either direction, to add a cable; pull a cable well away from its path and release to unplug it. Right-click cables, jacks or blocks to disconnect, bypass, remove or reset. Delete removes the selected block. CABLE SLACK sets how loosely the cables hang. Notes still sounding are released whenever the patch changes. Requires REAPER 7 or later (more than 64 sliders). Ported from x42 midifilter.lv2 by Robin Gareus and contributors https://github.com/x42/midifilter.lv2 (GPLv2+) JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A sampler from a popular video game: fifteen sounds (mario, mushroom, yoshi, star, flower, gameboy, dog, cat, pig, duck, face, plane, ship, car, heart) playable from B3 to G5 (MIDI notes 59-79). Click an icon to pick a sound (the icons are laid out in a grid that fills the window); the LIMIT VOICES switch toggles voice limiting (on: the original's monophonic/three-voice note handling). The on-screen keyboard covers the playable range: click or drag across it to play the current sound. Ported from unplugred's MPaint (https://github.com/unplugred/vsts), AGPLv3+. Samples and images by unplugred, CC BY-NC (see Dependencies/SuperColor/SuperColor-LICENSE.txt); samples joined into one stereo FLAC at 12-bit. Claude Sonnet 5.x was involved in the porting of the DSP and GUI JSFX code released under AGPLv3+ license\par}
}

